#!/usr/bin/env bash

lord --verbose << "EOF"
I've copied a single script called ./we-progress into the repo root. I want you to look at the overall structure of the repo in src/eu, and then create a small python package in a subdirectory of the repo root, call  bible. Give it a Makefile and a pyproject.toml. I want it to have a cli interface with a similar style to the one in the current repo (eu). However, unlike eu, the bible repo shouldn't be about my personal sex records, rather, it should be an interface for querying and plotting information about the books and chapters and verses of the bible that that we currently find in ./we-progress. I don't need the functionality of ./we-progress that does timing and says what book chapter and verse I should be on by any particular minute of the day. Rather, I want the cli, and the python importable, to both be about querying and plotting assorted stuff about the bible. We'll build the repo out over time, adding hebrew text and english text and so on. For now, just give it this functionality: 1. Add a subcommand 'bible list' that takes either a book name or a book chapter specification. For inspiration on how that should work, consult ./sefaria. Briefly, I want to be able to type either upper or lowercase names of bible books in whole or abbreviated, i.e., 'bible list gen' or 'bible list genesis' should list the number of chapters of genesis. 'bible list gen 22' or 'bible list "genesis 22"' (i.e., full or partial names, shell quoted or unquoted) should list the number of verses in genesis 22, one per line. Next: 2. Add a subcommand 'bible plot' that allows us to access a set of functions in a file called plot.py. It should support these commands to get us started: (a) bible plot chapters <book>. this should give a bar graph of how many chapters are in genesis, exodus, leviticus, numbers, etc. all the way through the end of the bible (we'll specify what we mean by 'bible' later). (b) bible plot verses <book-chapter> 3. A subcommand 'bible grep' that takes a regex and a book or book-chapter specification, and returns all Book Chapter Verse: <verse content> that matches the regex. IMPORTANT NOTE: All commands should support flags -o/--order accepting values [all,old,new,chr,jew,tor,deu,pri,pro,ktv,gos,epi,apo]. These should specify the following orderings and subsets of the bible: all = all books including apocrypha and pseudepigrepha, in christian order; old = old testament in christian order; new = new testament in christian order, chr = old + new without apocrypha etc in christian order; jew = old testament in jewish order (e.g., ruth goes later); tor = just the torah; deu = just the deuteronomistic history, including deuteronomy; pri = primary history: genesis through 2 kings; pro = the prophets, not counting samuel and all that, by prophets I mean Isaiah, Jeremiah, Ezekiel, and the rest, the ones written in poetry, and not included in the primary history; ktv = ketuvim, the writings, in jewish order; gos = the 4 gospels of the new testament; epi = epistles of paul; apo = apocrypha and pseudepigrapha. FINAL NOTE: I know the file ./we-progress doesn't have enough information to do all that yet. However, I want you to add all the FUNCTIONS described above, and also add all the KEYS to the relevant dicts and so on. The functions you can't implement yet with the data you currently have should return the NotImplemented object when they need an object to return, and raise a NotImplementedError when they need to raise an error. The NotImplementedErrors in question should all have a concrete description saying 'TODO: ' followed by whatever data needs to be provided in order for the implementation to work. For example, functions should sometimes call 'something = foo()', then check if see if 'something' is NotImplemented, and if it is, then raise a NotImplemented Error saying that such and such data is needed. However, if something is not NotImplemented, but then later a function 'bar(something)' is itself not fully implemented, then THAT NotImplementedError should specify that the data exists now, but the function is not yet implemented, and contain a concrete description of how a human or ai agent should proceed in order to implement that function. Do all that now. I've added a set of old scripts to ./bible/bin-scripts-to-use-for-inspiration-when-creating-the-new-repo. DO NOT just make the new repo a copy paste of those scripts. I want you to read what I've requested and try to implement it afresh, from scratch. However, if you need (i) data (ii) inspiration (iii) API endpoints, (iv) anything else you can find in ./bible/bin-scripts-to-use-for-inspiration-when-creating-the-new-repo, then please use that info however you find helpful, in order to make this new repo. Do that now, and keep working until the task is complete.
EOF
